ic rearrangement within the <italic>Antheraea pernyi</italic> silk fibroin gene is associated with four types of repetitive units</article-title><source>J Mol Evol</source><year>2000</year><volume>51</volume><fpage>329</fpage><lpage>338</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Sezutsu H, Yukuhiro K (2000) Dynamic rearrangement within the Antheraea pernyi silk fibroin gene is associated with four types of repetitive units. J Mol Evol 51:329&#x02013;338 <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11040284</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR19"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sukacheva</surname><given-names>ID</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Mesozoic caddis flies (Trichoptera) of Transbaikalia</article-title><source>Paleontol J</source><year>1968</year><volume>1968</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>202</fpage><lpage>216</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Sukacheva ID (1968) Mesozoic caddis flies (Trichoptera) of Transbaikalia. Paleontol J 1968(2):202&#x02013;216 </citation></ref><ref id="CR20"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sukacheva</surname><given-names>ID</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>New caddis-flies (Trichoptera) from the Mesozoinc of Soviet Central Asia</article-title><source>Paleontol J</source><year>1973</year><volume>1973</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>377</fpage><lpage>384</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Sukacheva ID (1973) New caddis-flies (Trichoptera) from the Mesozoinc of Soviet Central Asia. Paleontol J 1973(3):377&#x02013;384 </citation></ref><ref id="CR21"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Takei</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Kikuchi</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Kikuchi</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><s